{"id":10,"answer":"- From your notes (entry 5): your professor said inheritance is not for code reuse but for substitutability — "if B is-a A, then anywhere A works, B must work (Liskov)." That's the whole of what you saved; you also noted you wanted to re-read the LSP chapter.
- So your notes give the intuition but not the formal contract. What follows is general knowledge, not from your notes.
- Standard definition: objects of a subtype must be usable anywhere an object of the supertype is expected, without the caller needing to know the difference and without breaking previously correct behavior.
- Practical rules that follow: a subtype may not strengthen preconditions (accept less than the parent) or weaken postconditions (promise less than the parent); return types may be narrowed (covariant), parameter types widened (contravariant); it shouldn't throw new checked exceptions unless the parent declares them; supertype invariants must be preserved.
- Classic counterexample: letting Square extend Rectangle. A method written for Rectangle assumes setting width doesn't change height, which Square violates.
- Tiny Java-shaped example:
  class Rectangle { void setW(int w) {...} void setH(int h) {...} int area() {...} }
  class Square extends Rectangle { void setW(int w) { this.w = w; this.h = w; } ... }
  // void f(Rectangle r) { r.setW(5); r.setH(4); assert r.area() == 20; } // breaks with a Square
- If you have the LSP chapter handy, the formal version is usually stated in terms of preconditions, postconditions, and invariants (design by contract), often as "require no more, promise no less."","references":{"coverage":"partial","general_knowledge":true,"explanation":"One saved note (entry 5) directly addresses LSP, capturing the substitutability intuition your professor gave but not a formal statement or the contract rules, so coverage is partial. The other notes are unrelated Java/collections/DSA items or app feedback and were not used as evidence. General knowledge supplied the formal definition, the pre/postcondition rules, and the Rectangle/Square example, as flagged in the answer.","live_web":false,"notes":[{"id":5,"content":"Prof said today that inheritance is not for code reuse but for substitutability. If B is-a A then anywhere A works, B must work (Liskov). I want to re-read the LSP chapter.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790173527.6071672,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":true},{"id":26,"content":"Quote: 'The purpose of a system is what it does.' - Stafford Beer. Good counter to design intentions.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790174014.8878171,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":3,"content":"Java: ArrayList vs LinkedList. ArrayList gives O(1) random access because it is backed by an array; LinkedList is O(n) for get(i) but O(1) insert/remove at the ends. For my DSA exam I keep mixing these two up.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790173527.547001,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":20,"content":"Draft tweet: 'Your notes app should not just store notes, it should argue with you about them.' Too spicy? Maybe 'it should form opinions about them'.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790174014.7478125,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":6,"content":"Draft LinkedIn post: 'I built a local-first notes app that organizes itself in the background. Notes stay untouched; an interpretation layer evolves.' Needs a hook in the first line. Maybe mention privacy.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790173527.6940339,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":27,"content":"Bug I saw: in DeepNotes, if two structure reviews queue at once, one gets superseded. That is by design (coalescing).","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790174140.3680928,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":19,"content":"Question I need to answer: why does a Python default argument get evaluated once? Because def is executed once; the default object is shared across calls.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790174014.712186,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":23,"content":"Java collections: HashMap allows one null key and multiple null values; Hashtable allows neither. HashMap is not synchronized.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790174014.8086548,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":28,"content":"Java: checked vs unchecked exceptions. Checked must be declared or caught; unchecked are RuntimeException subclasses. try-with-resources closes in reverse order.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790174140.3861256,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":17,"content":"Java Spring: @Transactional only works through the proxy, so self-invocation bypasses it. This is the same trap as @Cacheable.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790173748.6797109,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":4,"content":"Java generics type erasure: List<String> becomes List at runtime, so you cannot do instanceof List<String> or new T[]. This is why generic varargs gives unchecked warnings.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790173527.5627732,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false},{"id":30,"content":"Feedback on DeepNotes again: I want to see which notes were used to answer, not just cited ids. The distinction between cited and related is subtle.","source_url":"","source_title":"","created_at":1790174140.478868,"session_id":"dab611ee-abe8-4b3c-987a-f749aa9eb9ac","revision":3,"excerpt_offset":0,"excerpted":false,"cited":false}]},"status":"completed"}
